// Generated by scripts/build_news.py. Edit app/news-data.js instead.
export const newsPage = {"posts":[{"id":"micronudge","date":"2026-11-14","title":"Ullhexa MicroNudge","href":"./plugins/micronudge/","category":"Audio tools/Plugins","summary":"Kick and bass alignment with microdelay, phase rotation, an oscilloscope and split-band volume shaping.","paragraphs":["For a long time, before Ull Hexa, I made a lot of psytrance-esque music. I learned everything about kick and bass alignment, phase cancellation, sound design, and whatnot. If anything, it is crucial in these genres to have great kick and bass phase alignment, as it carries most of the track. I used all kinds of tools, microdelay plugins, manually moving audio, checking phase relationships in oscilloscopes, and doing volume shaping in another plugin. It feels a bit frustrating, so I wanted to make one plugin that combines all three essential tools for kick and bass phase alignment.","1. A microdelay and phase rotator unit.","2. A built-in oscilloscope that shows the main channel and the kick on the sidechain input, as well as a summed display that shows how they look combined.","3. A built-in volume shaper with split-band frequency control, so that the low end can be smoothly ducked around the kick using a tempo-synced curve, while the top end can operate with other interesting or polyrhythmic patterns. The two bands have independent curve and timing controls.","Finally, I got it all in one place. Hope you enjoy it as much as I do. Cheers!"],"imageAlt":"Ullhexa MicroNudge showing the main and sidechain waveforms with its timing and phase controls","image":"./assets/news/products/micronudge.webp"},{"id":"glowing-flow","date":"2026-11-13","title":"Glowing Flow","image":"./assets/news/music/glowing-flow.webp","imageAlt":"Glowing Flow cover artwork","href":"https://open.spotify.com/album/1ZtEp6MYIUPgEjNuYpTwUt","summary":"Moog Grandmother, varispeed glitches, orchestral samples and analog saturation.","paragraphs":["This single has something Jon Hopkins-esque to it. I love music by Jon Hopkins, by the way. I ran a Moog Grandmother through the Qu-Bit Data Bender for some interesting spread-out, glitchy, varispeed effects. I also love combining orchestral samples, particularly by Spitfire Audio, so that is included. The low bass is also a particularly low bass, but going through the Elektron Analog Heat with some fully analog saturation, I pulled out some nice crispy tones in the mids and highs as well. Take a listen, if you will."],"category":"Music/Releases","links":[{"label":"Spotify","href":"https://open.spotify.com/album/1ZtEp6MYIUPgEjNuYpTwUt"},{"label":"YouTube Music","href":"https://music.youtube.com/browse/MPREb_a9MsmZBamvM"},{"label":"Tidal","href":"https://tidal.com/album/574030764"}]},{"id":"iiiipole","date":"2026-11-09","title":"Ullhexa IIIIPole","href":"./plugins/iiiipole/","category":"Audio tools/Plugins","summary":"A nonlinear four-pole ladder filter with drive, sample-rate reduction and four editable modulation curves.","paragraphs":["I love, LOVE, my Moog Grandmother analog synthesizer, and a lot of that has to do with that beautiful ladder filter. I wanted something similar in the DAW, a straight-up analog-sounding ladder filter with a nice big knob. The core uses four nonlinear filter stages in a resonant feedback loop, with soft saturation in the stages and feedback path, small differences between the stages, and simulated power-supply sag. The signal level, drive and resonance all interact with that response.","While I was at it, I also really like Elektron’s sample-rate reducer in the Digitakt. So I did some digging and found some instructions in some DSP build books on how that can be made. Further, it took the longest time to get the resonant sweep to sound right. I had all kinds of issues and screaming self-oscillations (not pleasant). But after a ton of fine-tuning I finally landed on something I was satisfied with. It sounds fat, as I like it, and I am pretty picky about my filters.","Given that I also dig Eurorack modules, I wanted it to look the same. Scanned some aluminium foil in my printer for the metallic panel texture, created some sweet knobs in Blender, and the look was done.","But how do I use it? I need modulation. I tried receiving modulation from external sources, but there was a small delay in the signal that made transients not sit right with me. So, I had to build a separate modulator panel. The curves run sample by sample inside the plugin, with tempo-synced modes following the project’s beat position and MIDI retriggers following the incoming note timing, so that, for instance, snappy psytrance bass plucks feel right on point. That took a while, but it makes sense now.","Each main knob has a modulation send fader in unipolar or bipolar mode. The modulator is an on-screen curve editor, synced or not to the project, and you choose one of the four modulation curves in the fader strip for the designated knob. Cutoff can combine all four curves. The editor is also something I have built over some time in another plugin coming soon.","In addition to dragging nodes around and shaping the curves, I also included a LOG/LIN/EXP knob for the curve interpolation, a bipolar SKEW knob that tapers either side towards the vertical midpoint, an overall amplitude LEVEL knob, and a vertical OFFSET slider, so you can get the curve in the right place much more smoothly.","The filter also includes DRIVE, which uses asymmetric diode-style waveshaping before the ladder, with an input-level-dependent ceiling and simulated supply sag. This adds harmonics and compresses peaks with managed level. There’s a high-pass filter towards the end of the wet chain, adjustable OUT gain and an input TRIM pot with clip-indicator diodes, a WET/DRY slider, and an end-of-chain FB control that feeds the protected wet signal back into the input before sample-rate reduction and drive. WET/DRY and OUT stay outside that feedback loop. Have fun!"],"imageAlt":"Ullhexa IIIIPole with its metal filter panel and modulation curve editor","image":"./assets/news/products/iiiipole.webp"},{"id":"headaxis","date":"2026-11-01","title":"Ullhexa HeadAxis","href":"./plugins/headaxis/","category":"Audio tools/Plugins","summary":"A spatial audio plugin controlled by turning and tilting an animated head.","paragraphs":["I love panning and placing sounds around in an acoustic space. But a lot of the time, I rely on doing so with separate functions and plugins to mimic that spatial positioning, as simple panning is not enough. For example, how do you place something above you? For this, I built HeadAxis. As you use it, the head represents the listener, and the sound comes from your viewing direction, i.e., you are the sound source per se. Drag and move the head around for the desired angle.","The plugin models several psychoacoustic cues that our mind uses to place audio in real life. Angle-dependent notch filtering approximates how reflections around the outer ear change the frequency response. In Headphones mode, this is coupled with tiny timing differences between the left and right ear, as sound reaches one ear first. It also models head shadow: lower frequencies diffract around the head more easily, while higher frequencies are attenuated at the far ear. Speakers mode uses level panning with more restrained directional filtering.","It’s simple to use, and to me, it sounds very realistic, much more so than simple panning. Give it a try, and happy music making!"],"imageAlt":"Ullhexa HeadAxis with its animated head and direction controls","image":"./assets/news/products/headaxis.webp"},{"id":"daw-txt","date":"2026-10-14","title":"Ullhexa DAW-TXT","href":"./plugins/daw-txt/","category":"Audio tools/Plugins","summary":"Session notes, task lists and reusable checklists inside your DAW.","paragraphs":["I got tired of losing my notes in endless paper notebooks, especially mix notes, so I decided to build a dedicated and capable note-taking plugin that I could simply have sitting in the Cubase Control Room insert section. This way I can take notes directly in the DAW and stay locked in, not having to open other apps. It works like a regular plugin, so you can place as many as you’d like in a project, on the mix bus, on separate channels, or as I prefer, in the Control Room in Cubase.","I have implemented a lot of standard key commands, redo/undo, several Shift and Command functions, multi-note selection and copy/delete, direct task editing, compressed, collapsed and expanded view modes, a personalized theme/color editor for your preferred taste of notebook, and other rapid and intuitive functions.","If you rely on a specific workflow, save the list in the list library and load it up depending on the project. Check off tasks as you go. I hope you find this useful, I know I did. Cheers!"],"imageAlt":"Ullhexa DAW-TXT with session notes and a task list","image":"./assets/news/products/daw-txt.webp"},{"id":"skakke-klage","date":"2026-08-21","title":"Skakke Klage","image":"./assets/news/music/skakke-klage.webp","imageAlt":"Skakke Klage cover artwork","href":"https://open.spotify.com/album/2RdrJmgLjSicfOpBnfbsgD","summary":"An empty paper tube sampled into a low, flute-like instrument.","paragraphs":["For this song, I created a sampled instrument from an empty paper tube roll. Recorded a stable pitch, did some cleaning up, and pulled it into a sampler. It is mainly the low-tone flute sound, which you can hear pretty early on. The rest was creative flow. Enjoy!"],"category":"Music/Releases","links":[{"label":"Spotify","href":"https://open.spotify.com/album/2RdrJmgLjSicfOpBnfbsgD"},{"label":"YouTube Music","href":"https://music.youtube.com/browse/MPREb_lzGWbV3PCed"},{"label":"Tidal","href":"https://tidal.com/album/551472284"}]},{"id":"space-cat","date":"2026-03-11","title":"Space Cat","image":"./assets/products/visualizers/Space_cat.webp","family":"audio","mode":"cat","summary":"An audio-reactive space cat with an animated face and a head bob guided by the beat.","paragraphs":["As I was in the process of planning out a game build, I got some ideas I thought I could put into an audio visualizer. It’s a cat face, with face parts meticulously carved out in Inkscape. The face parts respond to audio, and the head bobs to the music based on the tempo and beat phase from the clock under the hood. Oh, and every now and then some aliens arrive battling a space war, don't ask me why I put that there.","For some reason, detecting a rhythm/tempo in audio correctly was way harder than I first thought. Finally, after multiple clock builds, clocks reading other clocks, multiple clocks in parallel with evidence bias and input detection of various acoustic properties, I got a clock that works more or less okay.","The current detector uses onset detection in low, mid and high frequency bands, autocorrelation within each band, and cross-correlation between bands. It evaluates tempo candidates against rhythmic subdivisions and requires sustained evidence before changing the main clock.","If it still feels off, you can tap tempo with the space bar, using five or more taps. It should weight your input strongly and just micro-adjust the tempo and beat phase around that value, using the audio to refine the timing."],"category":"Audio tools/Visualizers"},{"id":"alien-trees","date":"2026-03-10","title":"Alien Trees","image":"./assets/products/visualizers/alien trees.webp","family":"audio","mode":"fractals","summary":"An audio-reactive fractal tree with layered textures, glowing fruit and falling rain.","paragraphs":["Another audio visualizer build. This one is a bit more trippy, and far less complex than Quantum String. It contains a central tree based on recursive fractal branching that responds to the audio, and some textured background with colour and texture variation based on incoming audio, as well as fruits.","Bass affects the branching angle, mids and transients affect branching depth, and highs affect branch-length scaling and sway. The background layers respond separately to overall RMS level, low-frequency energy and high-frequency transients, with the fruit layer driven by the high-frequency detector."],"category":"Audio tools/Visualizers"}],"totalPages":3,"index":"./assets/news/index-f13504f249a5138b.json"};
